g voltage-controlled gating in a potassium channel. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>118</volume>:<fpage>157</fpage>&#x02013;170.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11479343</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib22"><citation citation-type="journal">Fan, Z., and J.C. Makielski. <year>1997</year>. Anionic phospholipids activate ATP-sensitive potassium channels. <source>J. Biol. Chem.</source> <volume>272</volume>:<fpage>5388</fpage>&#x02013;5395.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9038137</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib23"><citation citation-type="journal">Fan, Z., and J.C. Makielski. <year>1999</year>. Phosphoinositides decrease ATP sensitivity of the cardiac ATP-sensitive K(+) channel. A molecular probe for the mechanism of ATP-sensitive inhibition. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>114</volume>:<fpage>251</fpage>&#x02013;269.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10436001</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib24"><citation citation-type="journal">Flynn, G.E., and W.N. Zagotta. <year>2001</year>. Conformational changes in S6 coupled to the opening of cyclic nucleotide-gated channels. <source>Neuron.</source> <volume>30</volume>:<fpage>689</fpage>&#x02013;698.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11430803</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib25"><citation citation-type="journal">Glowatzki, E., G. Fakler, U. Brandle, U. Rexhausen, H.P. Zenner, J.P. Ruppersberg, and B. Fakler. <year>1995</year>. Subunit-dependent assembly of inward-rectifier K+ channels. <source>Proc. R. Soc. Lond. B. Biol. Sci.</source> <volume>261</volume>:<fpage>251</fpage>&#x02013;261.</citation></ref><ref id="bib26"><citation citation-type="journal">Hackos, D.H., T.H. Chang, and K.J. Swartz. <year>2002</year>. Scanning the intracellular s6 activation gate in the shaker k(+) channel. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>119</volume>:<fpage>521</fpage>&#x02013;532.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12034760</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib27"><citation citation-type="journal">Hilgemann, D.W., and R. Ball. <year>1996</year>. Regulation of cardiac Na+, Ca2+ exchange and KATP potassium channels by PIP2. <source>Science.</source> <volume>273</volume>:<fpage>956</fpage>&#x02013;959.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8688080</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib28"><citation citation-type="other">Hille, B. 2001. Gating mechanisms: kinetic thinking. Ion Channels of Excitable Membranes. Sinauer Associates, Sunderland, MA. 575&#x02013;602.</citation></ref><ref id="bib29"><citation citation-type="journal">Hodgkin, A.L., and A.F. Huxley. <year>1952</year>. A quantitative description of membrane current and its application to conduction and excitation in nerve. <source>J. Physiol.</source> <volume>117</volume>:<fpage>500</fpage>&#x02013;544.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12991237</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib30"><citation citation-type="journal">Holmgren, M., P.L. Smith, and G. Yellen. <year>1997</year>. Trapping of organic blockers by closing of voltage-dependent K+ channels - evidence for a trap door mechanism of activation gating. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>109</volume>:<fpage>527</fpage>&#x02013;535.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9154902</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib31"><citation citation-type="journal">Horrigan, F.T., and R.W. Aldrich. <year>2002</year>. Coupling between voltage sensor activation, Ca2+ binding and channel opening in large conductance (BK) potassium channels. <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>120</volume>:<fpage>267</fpage>&#x02013;305.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12198087</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib32"><citation citation-type="journal">Horrigan, F.T., J. Cui, and R.W. Aldrich. <year>1999</year>. Allosteric voltage gating of potassium channels I. Mslo ionic currents in the absence of Ca(2+). <source>J. Gen. Physiol.</source> <volume>114</volume>:<fpage>277</fpage>&#x02013;304.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10436003</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib33"><citation citation-type="journal">Huang, C.L., S. Feng, and D.W. Hilgemann. <year>1998<